{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791394086,"nonce":"azz08ge3nmkaF/LE6NA9vWJuIhA=","title":"Investigate caller skeleton guard causing a cold glTF import cliff in hybrid mode","message":"ChimpMonk character deformation benchmarking found a reproducible cold-load sensitivity with the local Ash release. Adding one null guard immediately after RigImport.load changes the import interval from about 0.5 seconds to 20-33 seconds, although the guard is evaluated after the loader returns. The same guard in the later character constructor preserves validation and avoids the delay. This is a performance investigation; the compiler/runtime cause has not been established.\n\nReproducer in the sibling ChimpMonk repo:\n  python3 /Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/chimpmonk/repro/ash_gltf_cold_guard/reproduce.py \\\n    --model /Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/Blinc/examples/blinc_app_examples/examples/assets/3d/the_strangler/scene.gltf \\\n    --ash /Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/ash/target/release/ash \\\n    --out /private/tmp/ash-gltf-cold-guard\n\nNeeds Haxe and the two source checkouts, no GPU or SIMD native library. The external model is The Strangler by Jungle Jim, CC BY 4.0. The runner copies the same DeformationBench source into two temporary classpaths; only the guarded variant adds:\n  if (model.skeleton==null) throw \"This model has no drawable rig\";\nafter model.diagnostics.throwIfErrors(), inside the measured RigImport.load interval. Both variants retain the same guard in DeformationCharacter.new. Both use --preset game in normal hybrid mode, one warmup frame, one measured frame and one sample. Three default repeats alternate arm order. It keeps bytecode, stdout JSON and --jit-log stderr. loadMs measures through post-import validation; the null check itself is constant work. Do not use the one-frame deformation numbers as warm performance results.\n\nVerified paired run (one repeat, same current engine and benchmark sources): control loadMs 528.028, guarded loadMs 21972.075. Cold first frame was 242.998 versus 258.610 ms. Artifacts: /private/tmp/chimpmonk-deformation/guard-repro/{control,guarded}/bench.hl and 0-{control,guarded}.{stdout,stderr}. Earlier bisects: removing median array copying remained slow (19117 ms); removing the caller guard returned 464 ms. The original pre-optimization ChimpMonk engine also reproduced the slow import with the guarded benchmark (32795 ms), so this is not established as a new morph algorithm defect.\n\nAn initial comparison of different scalar/SIMD builds suggested a SIMD-specific penalty. Matched scalar/SIMD sources both showed the cold delay; that initial attribution is rejected. The paired guard reproducer uses scalar builds exclusively. Steady-state math/numeric checks pass.\n\nEnvironment: Apple M1 Pro, macOS 26.6.2. /Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/ash/target/release/ash reports 0.1.4, SHA-256 bf402f547bec3442eee5e2feb691f4708e03deda3a73bb9a6a589367465e069b. Source HEAD e217f5d declares 0.1.5; retest a freshly built artifact before attributing this to current source. The existing release artifact was used consistently, as requested for the benchmark.\n\nNext investigation: map the promotion traces to source functions, identify interpreter versus AIR/Cranelift/LLVM work during the cold interval, and reduce the importer/fixture while preserving the guard-sensitive call graph. Preserve output and exception behavior. Related investigations: cb93154 (LLVM admission), 2cea1dc (GVN clobber checks), 1fd2b94 (inlining/trap regions). ChimpMonk performance record: 97a45be.","files":null}]}